	We prove the statement separately for each collection $\ensuremath{\mathcal{K}} = \{K_1, K_2, \ldots, K_{\beta_r + 1}\}$. We know $\ensuremath{\mathcal{K}}$ is a collection of $\beta_r + 1$ sets, each of which has $n_{r-1}-1$ elements from $Y \setminus \rid{y_i}$ and $n_r$ elements from $Z$. 
	We argue that $\rid{-y_i}$ is uniform over all the sets in collection $\ensuremath{\mathcal{K}}$. This is because $\rcKupdown{x \to Y}{t, i} $ is a collection of $\beta_r$ elements disjoint from $\rid{-y_i}$ chosen uniformly at random. Variable $\rid{-y_i}$ is also chosen uniformly at random from sets with $n_{r-1}-1$ elements from $Y$ and $n_{r-1}$ elements from $Z$. 

